⏺ You're right — the throttle values are too high. Look at the numbers:
                                                                                                                                  
  - dm-23 (master1, throttled): 37.6 MB/s reads, 348ms await, 100% util
  - nvme0n1: 317ms await, 88.65% util — still saturated
  - VNC dead, host unresponsive — same symptoms as before                                                                         
                                                                                                                                           
  The throttle barely helped because 100 MB/s + 2000 IOPS is still enough to monopolize a consumer NVMe, especially since Proxmox OS lives 
  on the same disk. The cascade trigger is IOPS (small random etcd reads), not sequential bandwidth.                                       
                                                                                                                                           
  Cut the values hard — try this on master1:                                                                                               
                                                                                                                                           
  qm set 1010 -scsi0 local-lvm:vm-1010-disk-0,aio=io_uring,cache=none,discard=on,ssd=1,mbps_rd=30,mbps_wr=20,iops_rd=500,iops_wr=300,mbps_r
  d_max=80,mbps_wr_max=40,iops_rd_max=1500,iops_wr_max=800                                                                                 
                                          
  The logic:                                                                                                                               
  - IOPS is the killer — etcd random reads at 2000 IOPS was still enough to queue-starve the NVMe. Drop to 500 steady / 1500 burst         
  - Bandwidth drop — 30 MB/s steady is plenty for normal etcd (etcd DB is 46 MB total). Burst to 80 MB/s covers cold starts       
  - Proxmox host needs headroom — 15 VMs × 30 MB/s worst case = 450 MB/s, leaving room for the host itself                                 
                                                                                                          
  Then rerun the same write storm test. This time the NVMe await on other devices should stay under 10ms and VNC should stay alive.        
                                                            
