⏺ You're right — kubectl sends requests to the API server via HAProxy (your 10.0.61.100:16443 VIP), which load-balances across all 3
  masters. So the write storm hits all 3 etcds (every write must be replicated to quorum), not just master1.                               

  The IO throttle on master1 limits master1's disk, but master2 and master3 receive the same etcd replication writes unthrottled.          
                                                                                                                                           
  For a cleaner test that isolates master1's disk only, run something that stays local to the VM:
                                                                                                                                           
  # Pure disk stress inside master1 (no etcd/API involvement)
  dd if=/dev/urandom of=/tmp/stress-test bs=1M count=500 oflag=direct &                                                                    
  for i in {1..10}; do dd if=/tmp/stress-test of=/dev/null bs=4k iflag=direct & done
                                                                                                                                           
  Or if you want to test etcd specifically through master1 only, bypass HAProxy and hit master1's apiserver directly:                      
                                                                                                                                           
  for i in {1..1000}; do                                                                                                                   
    kubectl --server=https://10.0.61.10:6443 create configmap stress-test-$i \                                                             
      --from-literal=data="$(head -c 10000 /dev/urandom | base64)" &                                                                       
  done                                                                                                                                     
                                                            
  But even this still replicates to all 3 etcds — that's how raft consensus works. The dd test is the only way to truly isolate one VM's   
  disk IO impact on the host.                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                                           
  Your choice which to run — the dd test proves the throttle works mechanically, the kubectl test proves whether the throttle values are   
  low enough to protect the cluster in a real scenario.     
                                                                                                                                           
